<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sneferu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">26th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/26th-century-bce">26th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sneferu was the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s Fourth Dynasty, reigning in the 26th century BCE. He built the Bent Pyramid and the Red Pyramid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahshur">Dahshur</a> and completed the pyramid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meidum">Meidum</a>, developing the first true pyramids. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo-stone">Palermo Stone</a> records his expeditions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and the arrival of forty ships laden with cedar, probably from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sneferu.jpg" alt="Sneferu" width="320" height="550" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard Mortel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sneferu_portrait,_Cairo_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Sneferu, whose name means He has perfected me, was the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the Fourth Dynasty. Estimates of the length of his reign vary: the Turin Canon gives 24 years, now considered an underestimate, while Rolf Krauss suggests 30 years and Rainer Stadelmann 48. The Papyrus Prisse, a Middle Kingdom source, names Huni as his predecessor and calls Sneferu a beneficent king. His main wife, Hetepheres I, was the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khufu">Khufu</a>, who built the Great Pyramid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>. Among his children were the viziers Nefermaat I and Kanefer, Ankhhaf, and Rahotep, high priest of Re at Heliopolis.</p><p>Sneferu's building needed labour and materials. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo-stone">Palermo Stone</a> records his smiting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, with 7,000 captives and 200,000 sheep and goats brought back, the building of boats of cedar, and the arrival of 40 ships laden with wood. His campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> are credited with 11,000 prisoners and 13,100 head of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, and there was activity at the turquoise mines of Sinai. Such raids must have been devastating for the peoples attacked, and it has been suggested that the campaigns into Nubia contributed to the spread of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-group-culture">A-Group culture</a> there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Three pyramids are attributed to Sneferu, and under him pyramid design changed from stepped to true. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meidum">Meidum</a> the structure was first conceived with seven steps and later given a limestone facing to make a smooth, angled pyramid, though some scholars once credited it to Huni. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahshur">Dahshur</a>, the Bent Pyramid changes its angle of inclination from 55 degrees to about 43 degrees in its upper levels, probably because of unstable construction layers. Its top layers were laid horizontally, ending the step-pyramid concept. The Red Pyramid, his last, has a simpler interior, and fragments of human remains found in its passage in 1950 may or may not be his. Cedar for his building was most likely brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sneferu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Palermo Stone records that King <b>Sneferu</b> received forty ships laden with cedar wood, probably from this coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The pyramids and their complexes were the largest works of the age; <b>Sneferu</b> used more stones than any other pharaoh, and the canal to Lake Moeris, dug around 2300 BCE, was likely also the source of water to the Giza pyramid complex centuries earlier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-kingdom-of-egypt">Old Kingdom of Egypt · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27,29&amp;year=-2535" aria-label="Egypt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sneferu"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4924 5256h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4924 5256h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=27,29&amp;year=-2535">What was here in 2535 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
